t's trying to extend  its existing very intense censorship  regime to a new form of media so it's  telling people now that we see deep  fakes this is how all of the internet  platforms should be handling it they  should be trying to get rid of it or  they should be labeling it to make sure  that it is in alignment with the type of  information and content that the  government wants to see on the internet  so in on one hand it's not really  surprising because Chinese government  has always taken this very heavy-handed  approach to censoring Free Speech but on  the other hand it is still an  interesting rule that we should be  looking at outside of China because it's  clear that China has been following the  global debates around deep fakes and  around how it could fuel misinformation  and it has a adopted within its rules  some of the emerging best practices  around labeling for handling these  things and so it's sort of like a large  case study now on the types of  Provisions that other Regulators in the  EU or the US are considering right now  in terms of how such labeling Provisions  could be implemented how they could be  enforced and what kind of impact they  might have on businesses  back to that Global implication in a  second but  are applications that are based on this  kind of AI that we're more used to say  here in the U.S something like open AI  or lensa which we've covered on the show  before can you tell us about some of the  applications that do this in China that  might be subject to these rules  yeah so we have lens over here Baidu has  something similar I do is basically  China's major search engine but it also  does a whole host of other things like  it also has its own self-driving car  division similar to alphabet and in the  last year or two years while there's  been a generative AI explosion in the US  there's been a similar situation with  Baidu and other Chinese companies  building things like Avatar generators  or text generators or painting  generators that are more in the style of  like Chinese paintings for example so it  could impact these companies but these  companies the larger tech companies have  already been quite careful in making  sure that they aren't generating  sensitive content because they know the  rules of the game they anticipated that  this would become an issue so actually  what might happen more is that external  generative AI tools outside of China  that don't have the same limits or  restrictions they're going to start  seeing their outputs taken off Chinese  platforms or labeled by the Chinese  platforms when they cross into sensitive  territory how difficult though is it  going to be for Beijing to enforce these  rules  it's a really big question and that's  sort of what I think a lot of China  Watchers and Tech policy Watchers  outside of China are going to wait and  see because in general outside of China  deep fakes part of the reason why it is  so hard to regulate is because there's a  big question mark around enforcement but  traditionally some of those questions  around enforcement are more difficult  because platforms are trying to strike a  balance between free speech and  moderation whereas in China that balance  is not being struck it's just there's  always a leaning more towards censorship  when something might be in violation so  it's possible that it could actually be  really easy to enforce in that they just  get rid of a lot of content or we might  run into other problems that were  unanticipated let's talk about other  governments you may be watching what  happens with China and its rules I mean  are there plans elsewhere in the world  to take steps similar to this  so the U.S has been certainly debating  how to regulate deep fakes but at least  at the federal level there really hasn't  been much progress because of this  really strong concern about violation of  free speech there has been some  regulations or laws implemented at the  state level that ban deep fake use in  very specific instances in the EU there  is other efforts that are further along  in terms of specifically just including  the word deep fake and that language  into regulation but one of the issues  with Democratic environments is that you  can't specify Banning fake news which is  essentially the language that Chinese  regulation uses because if you specify  fake news without defining what fake  news is that basically gives authorities  wide latitude to take whatever speech  they want offline by just claiming that  it's fake news and that is essentially  the Chinese context now so with the EU  regulations say is essentially we  strongly recommend that platforms take  down deep fakes that might fuel  misinformation but there isn't really  significant teeth to that kind of  provision so having to be very very  careful with their language that's our  reporter Karen Howe Karen thanks for  joining us thanks so much for having me  Zoe and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing for more Tech stories check out  our website wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal thanks for  listening  [Music]  [Music]

